window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"path":"chapters/ch01-what-agentic-coding-is.html","title":"Chapter 01 · What agentic coding actually is — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron Chapter 01 · What agentic coding actually is After this chapter you can explain what a coding agent really is — model, harness, loop — run your first session without getting burned, and name the moments when reaching for an agent is simply the wrong move. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 The hype flattened the language. \"The AI built my app\" can mean four different things: a chatbot answered while a human typed every change, an autocomplete guessed lines, a scripted pipeline transformed text, or an agent genuinely acted — read the repo, ran the tests, edited files, retried. Those are not shades of the same thing. This chapter builds the mental model everything else in this book sits on. Three parts, one machine Strip the marketing and every coding agent has three pieces. The model is the language model itself — a system that predicts likely text, one token at a time. It has no hands. It cannot open a file, run a command, or remember yesterday. Left alone, it produces text and stops. The harness is the program wrapped around the model. You never talk to a raw model; you talk to a harness that injects a system prompt you didn't write, hands the model tools — read this file, edit that function, run this command — enforces permission gates, manages the transcript so the model appears to remember the session, and feeds tool output back into the conversation. Practitioners describe a coding agent as exactly this: a harness wrapping a language model with invisible prompts and callable tools, where the ability to execute code is the defining capability. Two people using the identical model through different harnesses will have wildly different experiences — the harness is most of what you experience as \"the product.\" The loop is what makes it agentic. The working definition this book adopts: an agent is a language model running tools in a loop, toward a goal, bounded by a stopping condition. The model proposes an action, the harness executes it, the result feeds back in, and the wheel turns again — until the goal is met, the agent stalls, or the budget runs out. Remove the loop and you have a chatbot. A chatbot answers; an agent acts. scenario: clear goal — “make these three tests pass” vague goal — “make the login page better” run one pass reset scenario: clear goal · context 0% · passes 0 0% 25% 50% 75% 100% = context limit pick a scenario, then pace it one pass at a time · the meter scale is honest — 100% is the hard limit · run BOTH endings and compare them: that gap is the lesson Notice what is missing from that loop: magic. The agent is not thinking in any deep sense. Each pass, it looks at everything in its transcript and predicts a next action. The craft of this entire book is shaping what the loop can see, what it may touch, and how it knows it is finished. Here is the loop you own — wrapped around the machine's loop. Every technique in this book lives somewhere in this second chain. aim one goal, one success test → watch skim the plan before edits land → verify run the proof yourself → trust it? → commit accept, correct, restart clean one pass of the outer loop · human-gated · every step stays yours tip Write the stopping condition before you write the goal. \"Make the login page work\" is a wish; \"make these three tests pass without touching any other file\" is a stopping condition. Clear success criteria are the highest-leverage habit in agentic work — trial-and-error loops need a finish line they can actually detect, and vague goals are how loops wander. What makes it agentic — and when to skip it entirely An agentic coding session differs from chatting with a bot in three concrete ways: the agent reads your actual files, runs real commands, and iterates on its own output without waiting for you between steps. Official documentation for leading harnesses is blunt that this is a different working relationship, not a faster chat window — you manage it more than converse. There is also a distinction practitioners lean on constantly. A workflow is ordinary code orchestrating a model through steps a human already understands: summarize this, classify that, format the result. An agent is the model directing its own process, choosing which tools to run and when. The canonical guidance from Anthropic's effective-agents essay: start with the simplest thing that could possibly work, and reach for an agent only when the task genuinely needs flexible, open-ended execution — agents trade predictability for flexibility, cost more in latency and tokens, and compound errors along the way. Add complexity only when measurements say you must. Say no to the agent when: the steps are already known — a script or workflow is cheaper, faster, and cannot improvise surprises; latency or cost budgets are tight — a loop of model calls is expensive by construction; errors compound dangerously — anything irreversible on live systems belongs behind a sandbox and guardrails first; you cannot state what \"done\" looks like — an agent with no success criterion cannot stop honestly, and neither can you verify it. warning Autonomy amplifies whatever it touches, including mistakes. Field reports describe agents that skipped permission prompts, stalled on slow tools, or wandered when a refactor exceeded their capacity — and the security story is worse: a rogue agent with shell access is a classic injection target. Treat any \"skip approvals\" flag as radioactive outside a disposable, sandboxed environment. Guardrails are part of the design, not an optional extra. Three classic failure modes These recur so reliably across teams and harnesses that learning to spot them by name is worth the price of this chapter. Failure one: the kitchen-sink session. Five features go into one endless conversation. Context fills up, quality sags, the agent contradicts decisions it made an hour ago, and nobody can untangle the diff afterward. Official best-practices docs warn against exactly this shape. Fix: one goa"},{"path":"chapters/ch02-the-harness-landscape.html","title":"Chapter 2 · The harness landscape — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral Chapter 2: The harness landscape Promise: after this chapter you can pick a coding harness deliberately — instead of copying whatever is trending — install opencode or Gemini CLI for exactly $0, and explain what skills, plugins, MCP and LSP do before you touch any of them. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · this landscape moves fast; the dates on every receipt matter First principles: what a harness decides for you A coding agent is two things wearing one trench coat: the model — the LLM doing the reasoning — and the harness : the program wrapped around it that turns a chat into somebody who can actually touch your project. Beginners assume the model is the product. Practitioners know the harness quietly decides most of the experience. Before naming a single tool, learn the three decisions every harness makes on your behalf. The tool loop The harness defines which tools the model may call — read files, edit files, run shell commands, search the web — how results feed back into the conversation, how many iterations are allowed, and when the agent must stop and report. Loop design is why two harnesses driving the identical model can feel like different products: one pauses to show you a diff after every edit, another barrels through twenty steps before surfacing anything. Your goal stated once, in words → Harness plans builds the prompt, picks tools → Model calls tools reads, edits, runs commands → Permission gate allow · ask · deny → You verify diff shown, loop repeats or stops the harness loop — owned by the tool around the model, not the model itself The permission model Every serious harness implements some version of allow / ask / deny : actions that always run, actions that prompt you first, and actions that never run. Where those lines sit is a values decision disguised as a settings screen. A harness whose default is \"skip the permission prompts\" is making a philosophical statement, not a technical necessity — and you inherit it unless you push back. Context handling The harness decides what enters the model's attention automatically: memory files, project instructions, skill descriptions, compiler warnings. It also decides what gets compressed away when a session grows long, and whether past sessions can be resumed or branched. This silently shapes output quality long before prompt-writing skill does — Chapter 5 goes deep on it. Four words you must be able to define You will meet these in every harness's feature list. Learn them tool-agnostically now: Skill — a written instruction file the harness loads on demand , teaching it a procedure (\"how we deploy\", \"how we name things\") only when relevant. Plugin — code you add that hooks the harness's own lifecycle events, enforcing rules or adding tools the authors never imagined. MCP — the Model Context Protocol, an open standard that lets a harness connect to external tool servers — databases, browsers, APIs — through one common socket. LSP — the Language Server Protocol: the machinery editors use for errors and \"go to definition\". A harness can tap those diagnostics so the agent sees the compiler's complaints directly. Watch the gate decide: a permission simulator No install needed for this one. Set the three gates below the way you believe a harness should behave, press run agent , and watch a three-step agent trace hit them one call at a time. You can even flip a gate mid-run — the next call obeys whatever the gates say at that moment . This is exactly what a harness's permission layer does for you on every single session, whether you ever look at it or not. permission gate simulator · interactive · runs locally, nothing leaves this page read_file(\"budget.csv\") pull a local file into the model's context ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny run_shell(\"python analyze.py\") execute a command on your machine ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny fetch_page(\"https://rates.example.org/current.json\") reach out to the network ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny ▶ run agent reset gates trace idle — set the gates above, then press run agent. The landscape, honestly compared Nine harnesses were alive and documented at writing time, plus one instructive casualty. The table compares only what the sources below actually say — license, the genuine $0 path, and the one thing each does that others don't. Nothing here is sponsored opinion; every cell has a receipt. harness license $0 entry standout opencode (Anomaly) MIT Free OSS binary; local models or free-key clouds; reuses ChatGPT / Copilot / GitLab Duo subs; optional paid Zen/Go 75+ providers; TUI/desktop/IDE; skills + plugins + LSP; plan/build agents Claude Code (Anthropic) Proprietary No free tier; subscription or API credits One engine across terminal/IDE/desktop/web; subagents, Agent SDK, routines Codex CLI (OpenAI) Apache-2.0 Free install; ChatGPT-plan sign-in or usage-based API Same agent in CLI/IDE/app/Web cloud Gemini CLI (Google) Apache-2.0 Free tier: 60 req/min, 1,000 req/day with personal Google OAuth Largest gratis allowance + 1M-token context Cline Apache-2.0 (JetBrains plugin closed) Free OSS; BYO keys / OpenRouter / local VS Code + JetBrains + headless CLI + Kanban board + SDK; Plan/Act toggle Goose (Block → AAIF/Linux Foundation) Apache-2.0 Free OSS; BYO keys, 15+ providers; sub reuse via ACP Native desktop + CLI + embeddable Rust API; recipes, 70+ MCP extensions Aider Apache-2.0 Free OSS; BYO incl. Copilot auth, OpenRouter, Ollama Git-native pair programming: auto commits, repo map, edit-format leaderboards Cursor CLI Proprietary · licensing unverified — check ToS before relying Requires Cursor account; free-tier limits not confirmed on the CLI page Editor-parity Agent/Plan/Ask in the shell; & hands a task to the Cloud Agent Pi (Mario Zechner) MIT ( @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent ) Free OSS; BYO keys via the pi-ai multi-provider layer Minimalist 4-tool core (read/write/edit/bash); TS extensions; deliberately no baked-in subagents or plan mode Roo Code — archived historical row Apache-2"},{"path":"chapters/ch03-models-without-money.html","title":"Chapter 3 · Models without money — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · L1 coral Chapter 3: Models without money Promise: after this chapter you can put a frontier-class model behind your agent today for literally $0, pick the right one of four free doors deliberately, and predict exactly where each door's published wall will interrupt you. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · every rate number below carries its own inline date — they expire monthly This is an on-ramp chapter, not an economics lesson — money appears here only where it blocks practice, and on day one it blocks almost everyone. Access is not one decision but four shapes , and every provider you will ever meet fits one of them: Ride an entitlement you hold. A paid AI subscription whose terms allow harness reuse powers an agent at zero marginal cost. Ride a published free tier. A vendor key with printed ceilings — requests per minute, requests per day. Pay almost nothing per token. Cheap open-weight APIs price agent habits in pocket change, especially off-peak. Run the weights yourself. Local models: no provider, no bill, no 429 — your hardware is the only wall. The transferable skill is not memorizing pricing pages; it is reading a published limit before you build on it, and knowing which shape you're standing in. Every harness from Chapter 02 reaches all four through the same three config fields — endpoint, credential, model id — so switching doors later costs an evening, not a semester. Feel the walls: the free-door picker Tables don't bruise you; walls do. Below are four real doors with their real published caps — nothing invented, every figure stamped with the date it was checked. Set a usage profile, then switch doors and watch the wall physically slide along the track. Push the daily slider past it and see exactly when your afternoon dies. One thing caps cannot show: free-tier data-training terms differ per provider and change without notice, so each door below prints its stated training-data policy inline wherever the source ledger holds one in writing — and every door without one is stamped plainly \"check current terms\". free-door picker · limit-feeler · published caps only · every door stamped with its as-of date · nothing leaves this page gemini cli · free personal google oauth openrouter :free never bought credits openrouter :free has ≥$10 credits groq free plan gpt-oss-120b local ollama your machine requests per day requests in your busiest minute 12 avg context tokens per request profiles: light practice · 25 one evening session · 120 all-day building · 400 team demo day · 1,500 no network wall — the limit is your hardware 0 500 1,000 1,500 2,000 req/day Three lessons the picker should have landed, independent of any vendor: neighboring doors differ twenty-fold (50 versus 1,000/day) for the identical request; per-minute walls bite independently of daily ones; and the local door deletes the wall entirely by moving the constraint onto hardware you own. Each door's date stamp also admits the quiet truth: these were the published numbers on their check dates . tip Pick ONE door tonight and take it end-to-end — practice beats planning. The four doors are interchangeable at the concept level (endpoint, credential, model id), so the skill built on any one transfers. The worst outcome is an afternoon comparing doors instead of shipping your first agent session. warning Every number in this chapter expires. Limits are repriced monthly, and doors die outright: GitHub Models — once a beloved free playground — was fully retired on 2026-07-30, redirects and all. Before betting a daily habit on any wall below, re-stamp it: open the linked page, compare the number, update your notes. The four doors, with their published teeth door published caps (as printed) as-of where it bites Gemini CLI free tier 60 req/min · 1,000 req/day via personal Google OAuth 2026-08-25 Daily reset; requires the OAuth sign-in OpenRouter :free 20 req/min always; 50/day without ≥$10 lifetime credits, 1,000/day once crossed 2026-08 Credit threshold; multi-account evasion blocked Groq free plan (gpt-oss-120b) 30 RPM · 1,000 RPD · 8K TPM · 200K TPD 2026-08 Org-level; token walls; cached tokens exempt Gemini API (key) free tier No public fixed grid anymore — live limits shown per project/model in AI Studio only 2026-08-18 You must check your own console before building Mistral \"Free mode\" RPS + tokens/min + tokens/month — shown per-org in the console, not on a page 2026-08-12 Numbers exist only inside YOUR console Qwen Model Studio (new user) ~1M tokens per model, 90 days, Beijing/Singapore regions 2026-08-13 Quota expires; \"Free Quota Only\" mode hard-stops with HTTP 403 so bills stay impossible DeepSeek API (paid, near-free) V4-Flash $0.22/$0.66 per M tokens in/out; off-peak halved; cache-hit input $0.007/M 2026-08 Off-peak excludes 01–04 & 06–10 UTC weekdays Local (Ollama / LM Studio) None published — and ≥64k-token-context models required for agent work 2026-08 Your RAM/VRAM is the ceiling Door 1 · Reuse a subscription you already pay for If any AI subscription touches your life, check whether its terms allow harness reuse before buying anything. opencode documents zero-setup OAuth for ChatGPT Plus/Pro, GitHub Copilot (device-flow), and GitLab Duo — sign in, and your existing plan becomes the model budget. Even a $0 ChatGPT plan draws from a shared Codex allowance pool, billed since April 2, 2026 in token-based credits. One boundary from Chapter 02: community proxies riding Claude Pro/Max subscriptions were removed in opencode v1.3.0 because Anthropic prohibits it — reuse doors exist where the vendor permits them, and vanish where it doesn't. Door 2 · Published free tiers, read honestly Gemini CLI's free tier remains the largest first-party allowance — 60 requests per minute, 1,000 per day on models with a 1M-token context window, unlocked by a personal Google OAuth click. OpenRouter's :free models publish their constants openly (20/min; 50 or 1,000/day by credit history). Groq's free plan is the instr"},{"path":"chapters/ch04-configuring-your-agent.html","title":"Chapter 04 · Configuring your agent properly — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral Chapter 04 · Configuring your agent properly Promise: after this chapter you can write a memory file your agent re-reads every session, add skills that load only when needed, and bolt on guardrails the model cannot talk its way past — so the same mistake stops costing you every single morning. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · config surfaces move fast; receipts carry the dates Fixing the same thing twice is a configuration problem Here is the quiet tragedy of an unconfigured agent: every session starts blank. The loop has no memory of yesterday except what you leave on disk. So when the agent uses the wrong test command, edits a generated file, or writes commit messages in a style you hate, and you correct it in chat, that correction dies with the session. Tomorrow morning it makes the same mistake again, and you pay for it again, forever. Every serious harness offers three layers of standing configuration, and the distinction between them is the whole skill: Memory files — prose loaded into context automatically at session start. They persuade. The model reads them like advice from a colleague, and can ignore them like advice too. Skills / playbooks — instruction files kept out of context until a matching task appears, then loaded on demand. Economy: capability without rent. Guardrails — hooks, permission rules, plan modes. Code that runs before or after the model acts, regardless of what the model believes. Enforcement. The leverage is compounding. A fix typed into chat pays once; the same fix written into a memory file pays every future session, across every project that inherits it. Configuration is the only part of your agent setup that grows more valuable the longer you use it — which is why this chapter comes before the fancy patterns. Watch instructions load: the nested-files simulator Instruction files nest. There is usually a global one for your whole machine, one per repository root, and optionally deeper ones per package or subfolder. When the agent works on a file, most harnesses walk from that file's folder upward, loading every instruction file it passes — and when two files disagree about the same topic, the nearest file wins. Flip the toggles below to create or delete files, switch which file the agent is editing, and watch exactly what enters its context. instructions loader · interactive · nothing leaves this page editing packages/app/src/payment.ts editing packages/lib/src/format.ts the repo — tick a file to bring it into existence ~/.agents/AGENTS.md global user file · every project on this machine monorepo/AGENTS.md repo root · shared commands + conventions packages/app/AGENTS.md nearest to payment.ts packages/app/src/payment.ts packages/lib/AGENTS.md nearest to format.ts packages/lib/src/format.ts what the agent loads into context 0 active instruction lines tick packages/app/AGENTS.md while editing payment.ts — watch the repo-root test rule get struck through · nearest file wins ties, global fills the gaps, siblings never load Three lessons fall out of that panel. First, global defaults are the floor : personal preferences live far from any repo and apply everywhere. Second, nearest wins ties : the moment a package declares its own testing rule, the identical topic in the root file is dead letters for anyone working down there — which is why \"but my root file says so\" is not a debugging strategy. Third, siblings never leak : the lib package's purity rules never touch app code, because the loader walks up, not sideways. This nesting-and-precedence behavior is specified, not accidental, as the receipts below show. tip Start with five lines, grow on evidence. Do not generate a 300-line constitution on day one — long memory files dilute the short ones and cost context every session. Write five lines today. Then apply the promotion rule: the moment you correct the agent about the same thing twice, promote that correction into the file. Your memory file becomes a fossil record of your actual friction, not your imagined one. The same grow-on-evidence bar covers npm-installed plugins, which execute their code at startup before you see anything — treat every install like adding an npm dependency to your own app and vet it first; chapter 2 owns the full supply-chain warning. warning Config rot is real and actively harmful. A stale instruction doesn't just stop helping — it misleads: last year's \"always use library X\" turns every fresh session into a fight against your own file. Date every rule (\"adopted 2026-08, because Y\"), review the file quarterly, and delete anything you can't explain the reason for. An instruction file nobody rereads is a liability wearing a productivity costume. The names you will meet AGENTS.md is the cross-harness attempt at one standard: an open, tool-agnostic instruction-file format stewarded under the Linux Foundation, with nested files resolving nearest-wins exactly as the simulator showed, adopted by Codex, Cursor, opencode, GitHub Copilot coding agent and Gemini CLI. Write one and several harnesses read it unmodified. Claude Code centers its own family instead: CLAUDE.md files arranged in a managed → user → project → local hierarchy, with @ -imports, path-scoped rules, and an auto-memory index called MEMORY.md that reloads every session under a hard load cap of roughly 200 lines / 25 KB — overflow stays on disk unread, which is itself an argument for short files. And Claude Code's documentation states this chapter's spine better than we could: memory is context, not enforcement — use hooks and settings to actually block anything. Skills: knowledge that loads only when needed A skill is a SKILL.md playbook — \"how we deploy,\" \"how we add a pricing row.\" Its genius is progressive disclosure: every session pays only for the skill's name and description (a line or two), while the full body loads only when the task matches. A frontmatter flag such as disable-model-invocation goes further, keeping a side-ef"},{"path":"chapters/ch05-context-engineering.html","title":"Chapter 05 · Context engineering — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part II · prompting craft · L2 olive Chapter 05 · Context engineering Promise: after this chapter you can keep an agent session sharp for hours — by managing what enters context, what gets compacted, and what carries across sessions. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · all token figures here are teaching approximations, proportionate to published measurements — your harness's real counts differ Context is a budget, not a bucket Chapter 01 established that the agent re-reads its entire transcript on every pass of the loop. That transcript — the context window — is not a warehouse where stuff accumulates harmlessly. Treat it as a finite attention budget . Anthropic's engineering essay on context engineering puts it plainly: the goal is finding the smallest set of high-signal tokens that keeps the task solvable, because attention shows diminishing returns as volume grows. The craft is not \"fit more in.\" It is keeping signal density high enough that the right facts win the model's attention on pass forty, not just pass four. The same essay names three levers for long-horizon work: compaction (collapse history, keep conclusions), structured note-taking (persist facts outside the window), and sub-agent architectures (do heavy reading in a separate window, bring back a summary). This chapter is those three levers, plus the discipline that comes before all of them: deciding what you let in at all. The evidence that length costs accuracy Why budget at all? Because measured performance degrades with input length — even on trivial tasks. A 2025 study from Chroma Research tested eighteen state-of-the-art LLMs and found accuracy falls non-uniformly as context grows, with distracting material compounding the damage. Read the sourcing honestly: this is vendor research, marketing-adjacent by nature — but it was controlled, published with public replication code, and nobody has produced a credible reversal. The practical takeaway stands: a bigger window is storage, not immunity. A million-token context (remember Gemini's free tier from Chapter 03?) will hold everything and still attend to it worse. Degradation starts long before the wall. What actually enters your context Before you typed a single word today, your agent's window already held: a system prompt the harness authors wrote, your memory and instruction files, one-line descriptions of every installed skill, and — the sleeper — the API schemas of every connected MCP server, because the model needs to know which tools exist and how to call them. Official Claude Code documentation itemizes exactly this loading order. Then comes what the loop itself writes: your prompts, the agent's reasoning, every tool call, every file read, every command's full output. Two kinds of load result: Standing rent — charged every session, whether or not you use it: system prompt, memory, skill descriptions, MCP schemas. Working history — everything accumulated this session: pasted files, tool output, back-and-forth turns. Feel the difference with your hands. Toggle ingredients below and watch the meter; the sharpness readout applies the context-rot finding (fill level plus distractor load) to grade how much attention is left. When history has piled up, press run /compact and watch what survives. Every number is a rounded illustration, scaled to real published figures — the point is proportion, not precision. context budget sandbox · interactive · token values are teaching approximations, proportionate to published measurements standing rent — /compact cannot touch these system prompt harness-authored, loaded first, every session 2,400 tok memory file your instructions; re-read from disk after compaction 900 tok skill descriptions ×4 descriptions only — bodies load on use (measured: ~68 apiece) 280 tok MCP server · fetch-lite two tools, tiny schema 700 tok MCP server · browser schema measured head-to-head at ~3.6k 3,600 tok MCP server · github warned in docs: can exceed the context limit alone 12,400 tok working history — /compact collapses these pasted design-notes.md medium reference doc 1,800 tok pasted payments.js source file, 300 lines 2,600 tok pasted debug-log.txt distractor-heavy — rots attention fastest 7,500 tok session turns none yet — each “work” press adds ~3,200 tok of reasoning, tool calls and output 0 tok standing rent history /compact summary scale: 200,000-tok window (illustrative) ＋ work a few turns run /compact reset session post-/compact floor right now ≈ 3,580 tok — that is what compaction can never reclaim. toggle ingredients · pile on turns · watch sharpness sag · then compact and see what returns Run the experiment that matters: load everything including the github server, stack on turns until sharpness reads HIGH, then compact. The window breathes again — but notice the floor. Now uncheck github and look at the floor line drop. That gap is the entire argument of the next section. Compaction: what /compact actually does When history crowds the window, harnesses offer compaction. The documented semantics: /compact replaces the whole conversation with a structured summary, then rebuilds a fresh context around it — your instruction files re-read from disk, so your rules survive even though your chat log did not. Any skill bodies you had invoked come back too, capped at roughly five thousand tokens. Hooks exist around the moment (Claude Code ships a PreCompact hook; opencode exposes an experimental session.compacting plugin event) so teams can inject continuation notes before the squeeze. Numbers vary by harness; the mechanism does not. Now the trap the sandbox staged: compaction collapses history only . Standing rent — system prompt, schemas, skill descriptions — re-presents in the rebuilt context, every single time. You cannot compact your way out of a fat MCP server. Harness documentation says this out loud: opencode's own MCP docs warn that a GitHub MCP server can easily exceed the context limit by itself, and prescribe disabling or globbing-off se"},{"path":"chapters/ch06-patterns-that-ship.html","title":"Chapter 06 · Patterns that ship — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part II · prompting craft · L2 olive Chapter 06 · Patterns that ship After this chapter you can name the five workflow patterns, pick the simplest one that fits your task — and run the book's own quality engine, a gauntlet loop, on work of your own. facts checked as of 2026-08-26 Chapter 01 gave you one loop: goal, passes, stopping condition, verify. This chapter is about shapes — the recurring structures practitioners wrap around that loop. Learn five names, learn when each earns its keep, and absorb the field's most profitable bias: start with the simplest thing that could possibly work. Most bad agentic work isn't caused by a too-weak pattern. It's caused by wearing a three-piece suit to dig a ditch. Six shapes, one baseline Before the five named patterns, the zeroth option: do nothing clever. One direct prompt, one session, a clear stopping condition. If that works — and it works far more often than beginners expect — you are done. Every pattern below is a purchase: more tokens, more latency, more parts that can disagree. Buy only with a reason. The five names come from Anthropic's building effective agents essay — the pattern-namers' own engineering blog, cited here as vendor material: Prompt chaining — decompose into fixed substeps, each feeding the next: gather → draft → check. Predictable and inspectable; worth it when the steps are genuinely distinct. Routing — classify the input first, send each class to a specialized handler. Buys focus; costs you a classifier to maintain. Parallelization — split one job into independent pieces (sectioning) or run candidates side by side and vote. Latency drops; disagreement becomes signal. Orchestrator-workers — a planner delegates to workers who cannot see each other. Strong on genuinely separable work; brittle when subtasks share hidden state. Evaluator-optimizer — one model generates, another judges against criteria, loop until it passes. The only pattern that manufactures judgment where no test exists. The pattern picker Four chores you will actually meet. Assign each a shape and read the scorecard: cost, latency, fragility, fit. The punchline repeats because reality repeats it — the simplest sufficient shape wins. One assignment below is deliberately, catastrophically wrong; go find it. pattern picker · interactive · scores are teaching heuristics, not benchmarks · runs locally, nothing leaves this page fix one failing test add a feature touching 3 files weekly dependency sweep subjective-quality landing page do nothing · direct prompt prompt chaining routing parallel fan-out orchestrator-workers evaluator-optimizer gauntlet simplest-sufficient picks found: 0 / 4 assign a pattern above — the scorecard lands here. tip Premature orchestration kills more sessions than weak models do. Before adding any stage to a workflow, name the specific failure it catches. If you can't name it, you're buying vocabulary, not reliability — ship the boring version. warning Verification debt compounds. Every unverified hand-off between stages multiplies into the next stage's inputs; by the time symptoms surface you can no longer attribute the failure. Verify at every seam — especially the seams you paid extra to create. Spec-driven development: rules before code When a task outgrows one sitting, the patterns need raw material: a spec. GitHub's Spec Kit turns that into a typed pipeline — /speckit.constitution records the project's non-negotiable principles, specify captures what and why while explicitly excluding technology choices, plan owns those technical decisions grounded in the constitution, and tasks breaks the work into ordered, checkable chunks. The launch essay's slogan is the right mental model: intent is the source of truth. The contributor deep-dive adds two corrections worth keeping: the constitution is what grounds the plan, and this is not waterfall — you re-enter any command when reality argues back. Right-size it. A constitution for a weekend script is ceremony; the simplest-sufficient rule applies to process shapes too. Scale the spec's weight to the blast radius of getting it wrong. Tests as the steering wheel The cheapest control surface for any pattern is a test the agent must satisfy. Willison's red-green guide reduces it to a one-line prompt: write a failing test first, confirm it fails, then make it pass. The confirmed failure is the point — it proves the test can fail, which is what separates a steering wheel from a participation trophy, and it prevents both broken code and dead code nothing ever exercised. Flip the order and meet the tautology trap: tests written after the code tend to encode the model's own misreadings, producing green lights that prove the code agrees with itself. Practitioner defenses: treat contract-style tests as reviewable specification, and audit suites for hermeticity — tests that fake their inputs verify nothing about the world. Evals before iteration When no unit test can express \"good enough,\" you need an eval, and Hamel Husain's field guide is the on-ramp: climb the ladder from unit assertions to human or LLM judging to A/B comparison — and know that missing evals are the single most common stall point in AI projects. One discipline matters above all: if a model acts as judge, align it against human labels on 25–50 examples first. An unaligned judge doesn't optimize quality; it optimizes noise, loudly. Reflexion, with an expiry date One peer-reviewed cousin belongs in your toolkit: Reflexion showed agents that verbally reflect on a failure — a short written post-mortem held in an episodic buffer — improve on later trials, lifting HumanEval pass@1 from 80% to 91% at publication. Treat those digits as historical: they predate modern harnesses by years. The durable export is cheaper than the benchmark — between attempts, write down what failed and why instead of blindly retrying. The gauntlet: the book's own engine Honest labeling first: the gauntlet is this book's house pattern, and it has no quantitative "},{"path":"chapters/ch07-source-literacy.html","title":"Chapter 07 · Source literacy for agent builders — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part II · prompting craft · L2 olive Chapter 07 · Source literacy for agent builders Promise: after this chapter you can stress-test any AI-productivity claim in about ten minutes — using the same three-tier system every receipt in this book was graded by. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · every claim below traces to the book's source ledger Hype arrives pre-packaged: a number, a logo, a chart going up and to the right. This chapter is the ten-minute routine for opening the package before you repeat what is inside — the book's own vetting discipline, turned around and handed to you. You will autopsy three real claims, dissect one famous vendor study, watch a benchmark get gamed three ways, follow one claim from launch video to public walk-back, and meet the machine that grades this book's own sources. Read a claim like a coroner Most AI claims die from one of five wounds, and you can check for all five faster than you can read the press release: No control group? Faster than what ? A credible number needs a comparison condition — same people, same tasks, randomly assigned with or without the tool. Without one, \"55% faster\" means \"faster than nothing, which we didn't measure.\" Who measured? Participants' feelings are not instruments; the gap between perceived and clocked speed is the best-documented effect in this field. Stopwatch, logs, or vibes? Funding disclosure? Whoever pays the piper picks the spreadsheet. Funding is not automatically disqualifying — undisclosed funding is. Contamination check? If a benchmark's problems sat on the public internet before a model's training cutoff, a high score may measure memory. Ask when test items were created versus when training stopped. Scope? A result holds in its studied population and nowhere else until proven otherwise. \"Experienced open-source maintainers on 2025 tools\" is not \"everyone.\" The professional skeptics Arvind Narayanan and Sayash Kapoor built standing checklists on exactly these questions at AI Snake Oil; the heuristics above deliberately rhyme with theirs. And when you chase sources, grade them before you trust them: T1 official docs, specs, repos, or established practitioners, verified before citing · T2 vendor blogs and aggregators, presumed marketing · T3 forums and social posts, leads only, never cited as fact without corroboration. The METR study, both acts You met this result in Chapter 01 as a warning about trusting your sense of speed. Here it is whole, because the second act matters more than the first. In July 2025 the independent nonprofit METR ran a randomized controlled trial: experienced open-source developers, real issues in repos they knew well, early-2025 agentic tools. Participants predicted a 24% speedup; afterwards they believed they had achieved about 20%. Measured reality: the AI group took roughly 19% longer . Data and code were public. Act two: in February 2026, METR's researchers publicly walked back confidence in their own method. Selection effects had crept in — skilled developers increasingly refused to be randomized into a no-AI arm — and follow-up work suggests late-2025 tools likely help more than the trial could show. Same institution, same public trail, updated conclusion. The lesson is not \"studies flip, ignore them.\" A source that publishes its own doubt, with dates and data attached, is worth more than one that has never blinked. Intellectual honesty is a behavior you can watch for. Anatomy of a vendor study Now the counter-example this book keeps returning to. In December 2022, GitHub published research quantifying Copilot's impact: developers completed a task 55% faster . Run the checklist. Who measured? The participants themselves, timing their own work — the exact faculty METR later showed is unreliable. Control group? None. Funding? The seller studied its own product. Sample? Ninety-five people in a designed experiment. Each tell fires. Fair in both directions: this does not prove Copilot fails to help — it proves the 55% figure, as stated, was never established. The contrast with METR's design is the entire lesson: same question, opposite rigor. How benchmarks get gamed Benchmark scores look like measurements and behave like marketing. Three documented failure modes: Gaming by leakage. The academic SWE-Bench+ audit re-examined the popular SWE-bench coding benchmark and found roughly a third of \"resolved\" instances leaked solution information and about a quarter passed weak tests; when the suite was tightened, SWE-Agent+GPT-4's score collapsed from 12.47% to 4.58%, and issues published after models' training cutoffs scored lower still. \"Resolved\" was doing marketing work. Gaming by contamination. LiveCodeBench attacked the problem structurally: it continuously harvests coding problems published after model release dates and measures performance on those alone. On static benchmarks whose problems predate training, models show large overfitting drops — proof that familiarity inflates familiar test sets. Gaming by governance. Epoch AI ran FrontierMath, a famously hard math benchmark, until January 2025 brought double news: OpenAI had quietly funded the benchmark and held pre-release access to problems and solutions before o3's record-setting run. Epoch itself called the nondisclosure a failure, said so plainly, and built a holdout set. TechCrunch covered it, quoting named actors. Notice what fixed it: disclosure, dates, and a locked drawer of unseen problems. The Devin arc: death of a claim in public In March 2024, the startup Cognition launched Devin, \"the first AI software engineer,\" with videos showing it completing real jobs on Upwork. Watch what happened next. Within a month, The Pragmatic Engineer documented that the framing had been walked back — the showcased jobs were not completed end-to-end as advertised. Independently, a YouTube creator posted a frame-by-frame autopsy of the launch demo: hallucinated fixes, unmet requirements, and a human replicating the demo task in 36 minutes a"},{"path":"chapters/ch08-good-ui-with-agents.html","title":"Chapter 08 · Good UI with agents — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 08 · Good UI with agents Promise: after this chapter you can get non-generic, accessible UI out of an agent — by feeding it constraints (tokens, component contracts, a screenshot loop) instead of vibes. facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · every claim traces to the book's source ledger Ask three agents, on three days, for \"a clean modern landing page,\" and watch them converge: white canvas, placeholder-grey captions you can barely read, one deeply familiar shade of blue, soft blur shadows, generously rounded everything. The models aren't coordinating. With no local constraints, a language model regresses to the median of everything it has ever seen — and the median of the training data is precisely the generic interface you didn't want. The inverse observation is the good news. Simon Willison's first-person account from the March 2026 Pragmatic Summit records that inside a codebase with established conventions, agents follow the existing patterns \"almost to a tee.\" Hold the two facts together and the whole chapter falls out: the pattern-following machinery is the product. Give it patterns and imitation becomes leverage. Give it nothing and the median ships. So we supply patterns — three constraint layers any agent can consume, plus the accessibility floor that converts \"looks fine\" into a measurement. Constraint layer one: design tokens A design token is a named value — a color, a radius, a spacing step — stored as data, separate from any stylesheet. For an agent it works as a machine-readable guardrail: the model doesn't have to interpret adjectives like \"warm,\" it resolves --paper to #F6F1E7 and stops improvising. Off-palette choices don't get argued against; they become unwritable, because every rule references a token instead of a literal. Honesty about maturity, per this book's protocol: the Design Tokens Community Group's format is published as a Community Group Draft Report — the de facto interchange standard for tokens, and explicitly, deliberately not a W3C Standard. Adopt it as the lingua franca between humans, agents and build tools, but verify your pipeline's importer before betting a release on it: { \"color\": { \"ink\": { \"$type\": \"color\", \"$value\": \"#201B14\" }, \"paper\": { \"$type\": \"color\", \"$value\": \"#F6F1E7\" } }, \"radius\": { \"card\": { \"$type\": \"dimension\", \"$value\": \"12px\" } } } Hand a file shaped like that to an agent before line one and \"make it fit the brand\" stops being a mood board and becomes a lookup. Constraint layer two: component contracts Tokens pin the raw materials; component contracts pin the assembled parts. The shadcn/ui docs state the quiet part out loud: open code you copy into your own repo, written to be predictable \"for your team and LLMs\" — an explicit AI-readiness claim, not a retroactive one. When a button, a dialog, a card already exist as readable source, the instruction \"use the existing dialog, don't invent one\" is enforceable: the agent can read the contract and compose instead of reinvent. Utility-CSS frameworks play the same game at a finer grain. Tailwind's utility classes plus theme variables act as a constraint lattice for generated CSS: every color the agent writes routes through something like bg-primary , resolved by the theme, so an off-palette hex has nowhere to land. None of this requires Tailwind specifically — the generalizable move is make the compliant path the path of least resistance . An agent greedy for the shortest output will take the lattice you built if inventing freehand costs more tokens than obeying. Constraint layer three: the accessibility floor WCAG 2.2's quick reference is the floor, not the ceiling. Two rows matter most day to day, both from Success Criterion 1.4.3 (Contrast, Minimum): text needs 4.5:1 , large text — at least 24px regular or roughly 18.66px bold — needs 3:1 ; the enhanced criterion 1.4.6 raises normal text to 7:1 . Contrast is arithmetic, which makes it the perfect acceptance test for agentic UI: no taste required, only math. The ratio comes from relative luminance: linearise each channel c ∈ [0,1]: c ≤ 0.03928 ? c / 12.92 : ((c + 0.055) / 1.055) ^ 2.4 L = 0.2126·R + 0.7152·G + 0.0722·B ratio = (L_lighter + 0.05) / (L_darker + 0.05) The contrast & constraint lab Every number below is computed live in your browser from exactly that formula — against this book's real palette, warts and all. Pick a text color, pick a surface, and read the verdict. One swatch is blue on purpose: it exists so you can watch the hue this book bans fail the test it deserves. Then flip the second switch and watch a token sheet rebuild a generic agent-produced card in place. contrast & constraint lab · interactive · ratios computed live from the WCAG formula · runs locally, nothing leaves this page 1 · text colour (foreground) 2 · surface (background) – : 1 contrast ratio ✕ fail ▲ aa · large only ✓ aa ★ aaa pick any text colour and any surface — the ratio recomputes live. Normal body text — the size you're reading now. Large text — 24px and up. WCAG \"large\" = ≥24px regular or ≥18.66px bold · thresholds from SC 1.4.3 (4.5:1 / 3:1) and SC 1.4.6 (7:1) 3 · the constraint switch Below: a quiz card the way an unconstrained agent ships one. Tap an answer to see its feedback, then apply the token sheet and watch the same card — same markup — get rebuilt by six named tokens. apply the token sheet reset sample question 3 of 10 Which gas do plants take in for photosynthesis? Nitrogen Carbon dioxide Oxygen Hydrogen Check answer quiz-forge token sheet · inherited from journey/design.md --paper #F6F1E7 · page background --paper-raised #FFFDF7 · cards --ink #201B14 · text · borders · primary buttons --muted #6F6455 · secondary text --l3 jade #1F9D6E · correct — ever with ✓ --l1 coral #D9573F · wrong — ever with ✕ radius 12px cards · 10px controls · 4px rhythm shadow 3px 3px 0 line-strong · never blur rules shape + label, never colour alone · no blue · targets ≥38px sample idle — tap an answer, t"},{"path":"chapters/ch09-testing-what-agents-build.html","title":"Chapter 09 · Testing what agents build — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 09 · Testing what agents build Promise: after this chapter you can verify agent output like a skeptical QA lead — ordering tests so they can actually fail, demanding browser evidence for UI, and running review gates you can execute without reading a line of code. facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · every claim below traces to the book's source ledger Agent output arrives pre-green. The diff looks clean, the demo works on the happy path, the summary says done . The trust-then-verify gap from Chapter 01 is widest exactly here, and this chapter is the closing move: the QA-lead habit of assuming nothing works until evidence says so. Three skills make up the habit — put the test in the right order , collect evidence instead of assurances, and build gates shallow enough that anyone can operate them. Verification is an ordering problem A test is an instruction to the loop. Chapter 06 introduced tests as the steering wheel; what it could only name in passing, this chapter makes you feel : the wheel only steers if it was bolted on before the car moved. Write the test first and it is a specification — the code must come to it . Write the test after and it is a biography — it can only describe what the code already does, misreadings included. That second path has a name: the tautology trap . Field synthesis of agentic development datasets found that tests written after the code tend to encode the model's own misreadings, producing suites that pass while the behavior is wrong — the test agrees with the code because it learned correctness from the code. Practitioner defenses exist (review contract tests as specification, audit for hermeticity), but the cheapest defense is structural and costs one sentence of prompt. Willison's red-green guide reduces it to exactly that: write a failing test first, confirm it fails, then make it pass. The confirmed failure is the entire point — it is proof the test can fail, which is what separates an instrument from a decoration. The same skepticism applies to the agent's own reports. An agent that ends with \"the feature works\" has asserted; an agent that boots the server, hits the endpoint, and pastes the transcript has exercised. Practitioner rule from the field: agents must exercise what they build and show the evidence, not declare success — and the corollary for you is that \"it passed\" is a claim, while a captured command and its output are a fact. The experiment: same feature, two orders Judge the ordering argument yourself. Below, two teams build the identical tiny feature: a page that divides two inputs and shows the result. Unknown to both, one of three defects rides along — a division-by-zero leaking raw Infinity , a negative input quietly losing its sign, or floating-point garbage in the decimals. Which defect is drawn is randomized each round, shuffled so repeated plays cover all three. Track A codes first and writes tests afterward. Track B writes the failing test first. Press both through, watch the same bug meet two different workflows, and let the scoreboard keep the honest accounting: bugs caught per minute of verification, across as many rounds as you play. catch-the-bug lab · one feature, two orders · randomized defect each round · runs locally, nothing leaves this page ▶ start round 1 reset scoreboard round 0 · defect undrawn · press start TRACK A code first · tests written after idle — waiting for round start. press: next A step TRACK B failing test first · code second idle — waiting for round start. press: next B step scoreboard · bugs caught per minute of verification no rounds played yet. A spends less time verifying; B spends more. Watch what each minute buys. Play at least three rounds so the shuffle deals you all three defects. Then read the totals the way a QA lead would. Track A posts the fastest verification time in the building and a permanently perfect green suite — and a bug-catching rate of zero, forever, because a test that merely restates the code cannot fail. Its green light certifies agreement, not correctness: 1 &divide; 0 shipping as \"Infinity — verified ✓\" is the tautology trap made visible. Track B looks slower on paper and catches every defect at step one, because the red run happens before the code exists and names the violation in the spec's own words. That asymmetry is the whole chapter: verification time buys nothing without sensitivity, and sensitivity comes from order. tip Make the agent write the check before the fix. One line of prompt does it: \"write a failing test first, confirm it fails, then make it pass.\" The confirmation step is non-negotiable — an unconfirmed red is a guess, a confirmed red is a measurement. warning Green ≠ correct. A test that cannot fail is decoration. Before trusting any suite — especially one the agent wrote after the code — ask the only question that matters: what change would make this turn red? If the honest answer is \"none,\" you are looking at Track A. Browser evidence for UI: the trace as a review gate Unit tests argue about logic; interfaces need evidence a human can eyeball. For UI work, the strongest non-coder-reviewable artifact in current practice is the browser trace: Playwright's trace viewer records a screenshot, DOM snapshot, network activity and console output for every action in a run, replayable step by step. You do not read the code to review the feature — you open the trace and watch what actually happened. That is a gate a non-coder can operate: did the right request fire, did the right pixels render, did the console stay quiet? Getting that evidence through an agent involves one genuine tradeoff, and Microsoft documents both sides of it. The @playwright/cli route is token-cheap: it deliberately does not push page data, accessibility trees, or tool schemas into the model's context, and it ships a show --annotate dashboard built for UI review. The Playwright MCP server takes the opposite deal — heavier context load in exchan"},{"path":"chapters/ch10-debugging-when-it-breaks.html","title":"Chapter 10 · Debugging when it breaks — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 10 · Debugging when it breaks Promise: after this chapter you can drive a disciplined debug — reproduce, hypothesize, bisect — instead of watching an agent thrash, and you know exactly when to pull the plug. facts checked as of 2026-08-26 Breakage is not the exception in agentic work; it is Tuesday. The difference between a beginner and a practitioner is not that practitioners break less — it is that their next move is scripted. Beginner: paste the failure into the agent, receive a confident edit, repeat until either the bug dies or the diff becomes landfill. Practitioner: capture the symptom, force it into a proof, cut the search space in half, and reach for the escape hatches early. This chapter drills that script until it is muscle memory. Reproduce before you explain A bug you cannot summon on demand is a bug you cannot prove dead. Before any theory, before any edit, get a repro recipe : the exact input, the exact click or command, the expected result versus the actual one, written down. Official best-practices documentation for leading harnesses encodes this as the first discipline of debugging with an agent — reproduce the issue, and fix the root cause , not the symptom. Those are two rules, not one. Suppressing an error (wrapping the crash site in a silent catch, returning a default value so the page renders) makes the pain stop while the disease spreads. The upgrade path from repro is a failing test : encode the recipe as an assertion that goes red today and must go green tomorrow. This does three jobs at once. It converts a vague complaint into a pass/fail switch. It stops the agent — or you — from declaring victory prematurely, because the proof is executable. And it hands every later tool, including bisection below, the automatic good/bad oracle they need. Agents will not do this unprompted; demanding the failing test is your move. tip Paste the exact error text, never a paraphrase. Not \"there's a type error somewhere in checkout\" — the full message, the file, the line number, the stack. Models route on specifics; a paraphrase sends them hunting in the wrong county. Same rule for your own notes: the exact string is searchable, greppable, and comparable after the next attempt. Escape hatches, straight from the docs Runaway correction loops are so predictable that harness manuals document the exits officially. Know them by name: Rewind to a checkpoint. Sessions checkpoint as they go; /rewind in Claude Code restores conversation and code to an earlier point, so a bad direction costs minutes, not an evening. Clear after two failed corrections. Official guidance draws a bright line: when corrections keep missing, stop correcting. Start a fresh session with clean context and lead with the exact error text. Hook-enforced iteration caps. A Stop hook can refuse to let a spinning loop continue, bounding the damage automatically. Caps exist because this failure is common enough to automate against. Beneath the hatches sits environment quality — Ronacher's advice from a year of agentic coding, and it holds: keep everything logged to one file you can actually read afterwards; keep the feedback loop fast enough to rerun after every change; prefer commands that fail loudly; and when context compaction hits a long session, abort and restart rather than nursing a degraded one. A slow loop and a murky log are how two-minute bugs become two-hour sessions. Bisect: halving beats walking Oldest trick in the book, and it survives agents perfectly. When you have an ordered history — commits, config edits, deployed changes — and something flipped from working to broken somewhere along it, do not walk the list. Test the middle item. Working? The culprit is in the back half. Broken? The front half. Either way you just deleted half the suspects with one test. Sixteen candidates fall to four tests, because ⌈log₂ 16⌉ = 4; a linear walk drags through up to fifteen tests and averages more than eight. Git productized the loop decades ago: git bisect checks out midpoints, takes your good/bad verdicts, and names the first bad commit — and git bisect run hands your test script to the loop so git plays both sides and you never click anything. Feel the race before you memorize the math. Sixteen commits below, one of them introduces a bug — re-randomized every reset. Pick a strategy, then click commits to test them. Par is 4. bisect race · interactive · runs locally, nothing leaves this page · bug re-randomized every reset strategy: binary bisection strategy: linear scan reset · reshuffle bug moves 0 · par 4 ✓ passes · ✗ shows the bug · dashed = ruled out, outside the suspect range If you played bisection even reasonably, you finished in four or fewer; the linear walk ground through eight-plus. That gap is the lecture. Note the prerequisite, though: every test needed a clean yes/no answer. Bools come from oracles, oracles come from failing tests — the disciplines chain. Reading the wreckage When the failure lives in a browser, the crime scene photographs itself — if someone remembers to shoot. Playwright's trace viewer records every action on a timeline with Errors, Console, and Network tabs attached, so a post-failure review is scrolling a film, not guessing from memory; teams commonly wire traces to fire on the first retry in CI so flaky failures leave evidence behind automatically. Driving the same browser through an MCP server adds live forensics: console messages arrive with source-mapped stacks and network inspection rides along, which means the agent can quote the failing request instead of describing it from vibes. Two habits make any of these artifacts legible. Read a stack trace backward : the top frame is where the error was noticed, the deepest frames are where it was born — start at the birth, not the notice. And keep Ronacher's one-file rule: when every session logs to one place, the question \"what did we actually do before it broke\" has an answer you "},{"path":"chapters/ch11-brownfield-reality.html","title":"Chapter 11 · Brownfield reality — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 11 · Brownfield reality Promise: after this chapter you can point an agent at a big, unfamiliar codebase and get safe, surgical changes instead of collateral damage. facts checked as of 2026-08-26 The repo you didn't write Every technique so far in Part III assumed canvas you controlled. Real careers are spent somewhere else: inside codebases that existed before you arrived, with years of decisions folded into filenames, half-documented conventions, and couplings nobody remembers making. Practitioners call this brownfield — built-on land, as opposed to empty greenfield — and agents change its difficulty unevenly. An agent reads faster than you ever will and does not tire. But it also arrives with zero inherited intuition: no memory of the incident that made the retry logic so paranoid, no feel for which of the four similarly-named modules is the live one. The hard constraint underneath everything in this chapter is arithmetic. A working context window holds a few hundred thousand characters at best; a serious repository holds millions. Nobody — not you, not the model — can hold the whole system in mind. Greenfield work hides this because the codebase grows alongside your understanding of it; brownfield puts you and the agent at the bottom of an existing mountain on day one. The craft is compression with judgment: build a small, ranked sketch of the territory, expand it deliberately where the change will land, and only then edit. Understand before you edit The tool-agnostic discipline has three phases. Explore: read-only investigation — find the files involved, follow the references, locate the tests pinning current behavior. Plan: write the intended change as a diff-shaped proposal — which files, which functions, what stays untouched — and check it against what exploration found. Implement: only now touch anything, smallest change first, verified immediately. Official best-practices documentation for agentic harnesses encodes exactly this explore-plan-implement arc for unfamiliar codebases, including delegating investigation to subagents so the main session's context stays clean for the change itself. The documented anti-pattern is just as instructive: infinite exploration — the session where the agent keeps reading, more files, more grep hits, another layer of indirection, and never commits to a plan, because \"understand everything\" was silently treated as the goal. You saw this loop's cousin in Chapter 01: a goal with no detectable finish line wanders. In brownfield work the wander wears a respectable disguise (it looks like diligence), so timebox it: exploration ends when you can state the plan, not when curiosity runs out. explore read-only · find files, tests, couplings → plan diff-shaped proposal · what stays untouched → implement smallest change · verified at once diff review understand-before-edit · exploration ends when the plan can be written, not when curiosity runs out Maps of big repos: ranked sketches If the whole repo cannot fit in context, what does? Every serious answer is some version of a repo map : a compressed outline of many files at once, spending its small token budget where visibility pays most. The reference implementation is aider's, worth knowing mechanically because the idea transfers to any harness. Aider parses every source file with tree-sitter to extract symbol definitions and references, builds a graph of which symbols refer to which, and ranks files by importance — files referenced from many places, and the entry points that anchor reference chains, rise; lonely leaf utilities sink. Then it greedily packs the highest-ranked files into a compact tree of paths and signatures, sized by a token budget set with --map-tokens . The founding writeup frames why bluntly: coding tasks need the model to find the right code, relate it to the rest, and change it — and finding-and-relating across a huge repo is what eats context budgets alive. What does the budget buy, concretely? Drag it yourself. Below is a mock 400-file repository, graph-ranked the way a repo map would be. Watch which neighborhoods the agent retains as you squeeze the budget — and read the prediction panel, which simulates what the agent would therefore KNOW versus MISS for one sample change: \"update the invoice tax rate.\" widget · map budget explorer — mock 400-file repo, illustrative ranking repo-map token budget: 1024 tokens aider default · 1k lean · 2.5k generous · 4.5k max · 8k legend: ● kept in the map · ✕ fell out (agent cannot see it) · ranking is graph-style — entry points and hub files survive first; hand-authored to be illustrative, grounded in aider's documented algorithm (see receipts) sample change: &#8220;update the invoice tax rate&#8221; agent would KNOW: agent would MISS: Read the panel like a forecast, not a verdict machine. At the default one-token-thousand budget the map holds roughly ten files out of four hundred — enough to know the shape of the app and find the edit site, not enough to see the spec pinning rounding behavior or the stale duplicate waiting to mislead. Even maxed out, eight thousand tokens covers barely half this mock repo. That ratio is the honest baseline for real work: the agent always flies part-blind; your job is deciding which blindness matters for the change at hand. warning Confident edits into unexplored code are how brownfield burns. The dangerous failures are not syntax errors — they are changes that compile, demo well, and violate a contract the agent never saw: an unmapped test suite, a second call site, a duplicate constant with different values. If the plan doesn't say where behavior is pinned, the edit isn't ready. A vocabulary for touching nothing Brownfield safety has a shared vocabulary older than agents: the refactoring catalog. Fowler's refactoring.com catalogues named, behavior-preserving moves — Extract Function, Inline Variable, Move Method — each defined so applying it cannot change what"},{"path":"chapters/template.html","title":"Chapter template — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · L1 coral Chapter N: Title goes here One-line promise: what the reader can DO after this chapter. facts checked as of YYYY-MM-DD Generalized first, always The technique is taught tool-agnostically before any specific harness is named. This section would explain the concept, why it matters, and the mental model — readable by someone who has never installed anything. tip Practitioner shortcuts live here. Earned, not invented. warning Common failure modes and how to recognize them early. grey zone · risk-flagged Anything ToS-adjacent or unofficial lives in risk callouts: what it is, why people do it, what breaks, what it could cost you. Never recommended without the warning attached. Demonstration on the build The same technique applied inside the current threaded project, with real commands, real output, and honest notes about what went wrong the first time. $ command that a reader can paste verbatim expected output shown honestly, including errors Receipts Every factual claim above traces to a source card like these: T1 official https://opencode.ai/docs/skills checked 2026-08-24 T2 vendor blog https://example.com/marketing-post checked 2026-08-24 · marketing claims flagged inline T3 lead only https://example.com/forum-rumor never cited as fact — pointer for your own verification Try it yourself Exact-click checkpoint. Open X, click Y — written so a non-coder follows mechanically. Break it on purpose. Change one thing, predict the result, check your prediction. Ship-sized task. Apply today's technique to your own project, not just the book's. Self-check Comprehension question one? Answer, in plain language. Comprehension question two? Answer. When would this technique be the wrong choice? Answer. Beyond this build Where else this pattern applies, which other harnesses support it (and how their mechanics differ), and the edge cases where it breaks down. This block is the generalization guarantee — never omit it. ← contents next chapter →"},{"path":"index.html","title":"The Agentic Coding Field Manual — अ Man Learns Things","text":"अ Man Learns Things · a field manual · scaffold v1 The Agentic Coding Field Manual Master the modern agentic workflow — prompting patterns that survive production, UI craft, testing discipline, daily habits — with coding agents like opencode, Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI. $0-first tools, receipts behind every claim. Written for people who learn by building, not by reading theory. Open the contents Watch it being built 16 chapters 3 real builds $0-first tools blind-critic quality gates The method that wrote this book Every chapter is ground through the same loop you'll learn inside it: a builder agent makes a piece, a separate critic with fresh context compares ours against the real reference blind, picks a winner, names the biggest gap, and sends it back. The loop only stops when ours wins. builder writes the piece → critic fresh context, harsh, binary blind A/B vs bar → biggest gap named, sent back loop until ours wins — never a round count Three real builds carry the book You won't practice on toys. Three projects from Aman's own build specs get constructed chapter by chapter as the book teaches — each one $0-native, each one shipped. build 1 · weekend Agent Cost Compass Paste your agent's token pattern, see what a run costs across ~20 LLM providers. Your first agent build — configured, prompted, verified, shipped. build 2 · two-week NCERT Quiz Forge Quizzes + spaced repetition for NCERT textbooks. Where UI craft, testing discipline and human-review gates get learned for real. build 3 · six-week capstone Free-Tier Atlas The IMDb of free tiers — change-tracking, watchlists, community submissions. Pipelines, anti-bloat ops and honest grey zones, at capstone scale. Generalized, not project-bound Every chapter teaches the technique tool-agnostically first, demonstrates it on the build, then closes with Beyond this build : where else the pattern applies, which other tools it works with, and where it breaks. The coverage matrix in docs/ guarantees nothing gets dropped because a project didn't happen to need it."},{"path":"progress.html","title":"Progress — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Progress The book is built in phases, each phase a fresh gauntlet run that resumes from disk. This board is the human view of STATUS.md — agents update both together at every milestone. last updated: 2026-08-25 · P1 research wave complete Done P0 · shipped Scaffold Repo + local git, design tokens (light + dark), site skeleton, client-side search, theme toggle, coverage matrix, governance files. P1 · shipped Research wave Six research agents vetted sources per chapter cluster. ~160 tiered rows in the source ledger; every future citation comes from it. Ten gaps logged with binding drafting instructions. P2 · wave 1 · shipped Chapters 01–02 won their gauntlets Ch 01 took three rounds — the critic forced an interactive loop lab where a clear goal exits honestly at 36% context and a vague one dies at the 96% wall. Ch 02 won in two with a permission-gate simulator you can flip mid-run. Build 1 M1 scaffolded at Projects\\agent-cost-compass. In transit P2 · shipped Foundations complete — all four chapters won Ch 01 loop lab (3 rounds) · Ch 02 permission simulator (2 rounds) · Ch 03 limit-feeler with moving 429 walls · Ch 04 instructions-loader making nested memory-file precedence visible. Build 1 code-complete locally through M3: 19 attributed models, math verifier green, review round caught and fixed a real ledger error (DeepSeek off-peak semantics, corrected against the live page). In transit gate · your turn Retroactive verification packets Build 1's milestone packets wait in BUILD.md change log — five-minute hand-checks whenever you like. Nothing blocks P3. P3 · next Craft chapters + Build 2 Chapters 05–11 (context engineering, patterns, UI, testing, debugging, brownfield) through the same gauntlet; ncert-quiz-forge starts its build. Not yet built P4 Ship & sanity + Build 3 Chapters 12–16; free-tier-atlas capstone; Build 1's cron/diff milestones land here too (they need the GitHub remote that only exists after P5 opens). P3 Craft + Build 2 Chapters 5–11; ncert-quiz-forge built. P4 Ship & sanity + Build 3 Chapters 12–16; free-tier-atlas capstone. P5 Integration & launch Cross-links, full-book blind judging vs both bars, push to GitHub, Pages live. How quality is enforced builder agent fresh context → critic agent fetches real bars blind A/B, labels stripped → verdict winner + biggest gap bars: ciechanow.ski (visual) · code.claude.com best practices (content)"},{"path":"toc.html","title":"Contents — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Contents Six parts on six route lines. Statuses update as phases land; rows become links when their chapter ships. part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron 01 What agentic coding actually is v1 · won its gauntlet Harness vs model vs loop. Mental models that survive contact with real work, the failure modes nobody blogs about, and when NOT to reach for an agent. Includes an interactive loop lab: run the same bug with a clear goal vs a vague one. part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral 02 The harness landscape v1 · won its gauntlet opencode deep-dive; Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Cline, Goose, Aider, Cursor CLI, Pi compared on what matters at $0. Includes a permission-gate simulator you can flip mid-run. 03 Models without money v1 · won its gauntlet Four $0 doors into frontier-class models - subscription reuse, free tiers, cheap open-weight APIs, fully local - with a limit-feeler simulator: drag your usage and watch each door's 429 wall move. 04 Configuring your agent properly v1 · won its gauntlet Memory files, skills and guardrails as compounding leverage - with an instructions-loader that makes nested AGENTS.md precedence visible when you toggle files into existence. part II · prompting craft · L2 olive 05 Context engineering planned What enters context, compaction, memory across sessions, token discipline, context rot and how to fight it. 06 Patterns that ship planned Plan-first, spec-driven development, gauntlet loops and evaluator patterns, TDD with agents, subagent fan-out, verification loops, anti-patterns. 07 Source literacy for agent builders planned Reliable vs unreliable sources, how benchmarks get gamed, verifying claims yourself in minutes. part III · building real things · L3 jade 08 Good UI with agents planned Design tokens and systems, screenshot-compare loops, component contracts, accessibility as a default not an afterthought. 09 Testing what agents build planned Test strategy for agent output, browser automation, review gates a non-coder can run, evals-lite. 10 Debugging when it breaks planned Systematic debugging with agents, bisection, reading logs, escape hatches when the loop spins. 11 Brownfield reality planned Working inside large existing codebases: understand-before-edit, safe refactors, mapping unfamiliar territory. part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum 12 Pipelines that prevent bloat planned Branching discipline, CI gates, agent code review, dependency hygiene, refactor loops, docs drift. 13 Shipping for $0 planned GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel free paths, preview deploys, release notes written by agents. 14 Security & secrets planned Prompt injection, secret leakage into context, malicious MCP servers and skill supply chains, permission deny lists. 15 Costs, limits & grey zones planned One compact reference chapter: rate-limit math, spend caps, free-tier tradeoffs and ToS boundaries — the honest footnote, not the theme. 16 Your daily agentic workflow planned The capstone habit: session rituals, memory upkeep, parallel worktrees, review rhythm — how competent people actually work with agents every day. appendices · reference shelf · L5 rose A Tool cheat sheet planned One-page-per-harness command and config reference. B The source ledger planned Every source cited in the book, tiered and dated. C Glossary planned Harness, context window, MCP, hook, skill — plain definitions. D Coverage matrix planned Dimensions × chapters — proof of exhaustiveness, living document."}];
